   "landmarkRmsPct": 2.812,
   "hullIou": 0.6828
  },
  "note": "Accepted with --keep against a -0.10 scoreboard, on evidence the scoreboard cannot carry. The old crops were pictures: extract_pbr2.py grew each located rect to the extractor 96 px minimum, so pbr/crops/skin.png was a whole eye, cloth was the chest buckles, leather was a boot. Those got stretched over every component of that material, which is most of the collage look the user objected to. Region evidence for the change: hair_mass dE 25.9 -> 17.2, braids 22.4 -> 21.3. The regressions are all skin BANDS -- midriff, arms, chest_top -- and the band is not skin: the reference midriff band reads L 39.3 while its bare skin medians L 69.6, because the band is mostly garment. My garment covers too little area, so honest skin makes the band too bright. That is a coverage defect the torso patch owns, not a colour defect, and keeping smeared crops would only hide it -- every geometry measurement after this would be taken through mush. Two genuine bugs were fixed on the way: skin was targeted from head_1 where the face is a lit close-up, and skinShade was banded over the black top and came back 130 dLab from its target."
 },
